I appreciate you, and I want you to know it!
The first factor that determines an employee’s job satisfaction is not salary but whether they feel sufficiently valued and appreciated in their work.
According to Maslow’s hierarchy of needs, after the basic survival needs are met, the most urgent needs are psychological needs – the desire to be understood, acknowledged, and appreciated.
Indeed, for the Chinese people, most of them “love you in my heart but can’t say it out loud.” Even if they have love, gratitude, and admiration, they keep these feelings inside without saying anything.
In this book, the author discusses five languages of appreciation: affirmative words, thoughtful moments, acts of service, gifts of appreciation, and physical contact.
I think it is not important that you know what the five languages of appreciation are, but that you can use them from within, anytime, anywhere, to express appreciation and gratitude to those around you, whether they are family, friends or colleagues.
In fact, the closer we are to someone, the more we need to appreciate and be grateful, so that our lives become better.
Appreciation can help companies retain talent to some extent and reduce turnover rates.
Thanks to the translator for bringing us wonderful foreign ideas and worlds.
Due to cultural backgrounds in China and Western countries, there is likely to be some difference between languages, so if you have an opportunity to see the original version of the author, I believe you will gain more.
Thanks again to the authors, translators, and editors for their hard work!
