Not very recommended reading
I read this a long time ago, and I just happened to come across it and write a review.
This book is not so much a collection of short stories as it is a collection of fragments of life about love.
The writing style of this book is extremely dull, like plain water, and the phrasing is to the extreme in pursuit of simplicity. The plot is as quiet and dull as a street in the afternoon of summer.
Each short story is a snapshot of a small period in everyday life, without an opening or an ending.
Finally, they were collected together under the title What We Are Talking About When We Talk About Love.
I think that this collection of novels, and its title, is like an act of art, in which the author wants to express that love is more than a tragic union on stage, a dry sea in urban legends, or a cold-blooded one-on-one, that love is more than that and should not be represented by that, but that it is also a life of watery plains, a dull day after day, but also a life of helplessness, an escape from reality, and uncertainty about the future.
As you read this book, memories of arguing couples beside you, uninteresting marriages with acquaintances, and unsatisfactory relationships may come to mind.
This is the reason why I do not recommend reading it. From the aesthetic point of view, the writing and plot of the book are tasteless, unless you live in a world of turbulent waves or a world of sweetness like living in a candy castle. Otherwise, you will feel that it is strange and boring to read it.
And in terms of depth of thought, everyday situations do not surprise or expand one’s mind and perspective (this is unlike in Gabriel Garcia Marquez’s Love in the Time of Cholera), while brief episodes fail to address any issues, such as what kind of mistake led to the failure of love?
What causes the breakup of the main character's marriage?
So I don't recommend reading this book, whether you read it or not doesn't matter much.
The author's ideas are expressed more through techniques and genres than through narrative, which is also a reason for the poor reading experience.
